WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.450
On the same day as the attack on Pearl Harbor,
Japan carried out a combined attack against

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:13.210
Hong Kong, the Philippines, Thailand and Malaysia.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:17.880
That day was December 7th, 1941.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.650
After taking control of Hong Kong, the Japanese
army imprisoned the colony’s administrators

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:28.240
and many British and American citizens in
prison camps, replaced the Hong Kong dollar

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.119
with the military Yen and confiscated all
of the city’s manufacturing and commercial

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:35.129
facilities.

00:00:35.129 --> 00:00:39.520
During the four years of Japanese occupation,
Hong Kong’s economy plummeted so low it

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.989
was virtually ruined and the population was
reduced by half.

00:00:42.989 --> 00:00:50.600
But finally on August 15th, 1945, after the
harsh Pacific War, the Second World War ended.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.420
Japan surrendered and Hong Kong was returned
to the British.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.800
From that moment on, a new story began.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.370
It was a time for economic takeoff.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:03.729
A process that would transform this city into
one of the freest and richest territories

00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:15.950
in all of Asia and, indeed, the entire planet.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:20.619
With seven and a half million inhabitants
and more than 1,200 skyscrapers, Hong Kong

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:25.060
has been the great benchmark of almost the
entire Asian continent for the past couple

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:26.060
of decades.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.619
However, this is changing.

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:35.219
You see, located in the middle of the Pearl
River delta, China’s great technological

00:01:35.219 --> 00:01:38.630
and industrial area, is the city of Shenzhen.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:42.490
In 2018, Hong Kong saw its neighbor, Shenzhen
– known as the Chinese Silicon Valley – snatch

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:46.250
first place as the city with the largest economy
in southern China.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:50.800
That's right, Hong Kong is no longer the biggest
economy of the cities that make up the Pearl

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.270
River delta.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.560
But, this has a small caveat.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.409
Shenzhen has just over ten million inhabitants,
which is three to five million more than Hong

00:02:00.409 --> 00:02:04.740
Kong, so in terms of wealth per capita, Hong
Kong is still much richer.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:10.259
But, that’s beside the point because what
this shows us is that things are changing,

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:12.360
and Hong Kong is falling behind.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.140
And that doesn’t mean to say that the city’s
economy isn’t growing.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.650
Just that Hong Kong’s neighbors are growing
at a faster rate.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:21.140
That and the fact that while its neighbors,
particularly Shenzhen, have become important

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:25.370
technological and industrial centers, from
which multinationals as influential as Tencent,

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:30.830
DJI or Huawei have emerged, Hong Kong is only
significant as a financial center... and nothing

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:31.830
more.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.640
And the question that more and more people
are asking themselves today is, is this a

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.530
sign of Hong Kong’s decline or rebirth?

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.710
Yes, you heard that correctly.

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.250
It’s black or white.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:45.880
Listen up.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.520
THE CITY OF CONTRASTS)

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.760
Folks, it’s your turn for a question.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.150
Are you ready?

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:58.260
Which city do you think has the most ultra-rich
people living in it?

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.630
The city with the highest number of people
with assets worth $30 million or more?

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:07.820
I’m guessing many of you thought of New
York, London or perhaps Dubai, right?

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.740
Well... cold, cold.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:10.740
Freezing!

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:14.710
New York eclipsed as city with most extremely
wealthy people.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:19.880
Hong Kong is now home to more extremely rich
people than any other city in the world.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:20.880
Washington Post)

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:21.880
Yes, Hong Kong.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.600
Hong Kong, is the city with the most billionaires
on the planet right now.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.470
More than 10,000 residents in this city have
a portfolio worth more than $30 million.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:33.330
What do you think of that?

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:38.900
So you may be thinking... well… if the city’s
growing at around 3% per year and it’s the

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:42.800
city with the most billionaires in the world...
then things are going really well, right?

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:49.430
Well yes... and no.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:52.460
Folks, Hong Kong is a paradox.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:56.560
See, on the one hand we have an apparently
thriving and prosperous city.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:59.140
A modern and influential financial center.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.640
To give you an idea, Hong Kong’s Stock Exchange
is huge.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:07.300
It lists more than 2,300 companies with a
total market value of just over $4.1 trillion.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:12.510
However, at the same time, this city has loads
of problems.

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:18.380
The poorest residents are earning progressively
less and less money, infant mortality rates

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:23.000
are rising, the population is aging rapidly
and a huge percentage of the population lives

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.090
in housing that falls well short of building
standards in any developed country.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:31.350
In many cases these are tiny spaces that don’t
even have windows or ventilation.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:34.720
These could truly be described as holes.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:40.400
Folks, the most claustrophobic living spaces
in the world can be found in this city.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.000
And this has a lot to do with the fact that
what should be a well-off middle class, really

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.260
isn’t.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:53.090
Nearly half of Hong Kong flats rent for US$2,550
a month – 70 per cent of median household

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.090
income.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:55.090
SCPM)

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:59.410
According to research company Demographia,
housing prices are 20.9 times higher than

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:04.700
the average household's annual income in Hong
Kong, compared with 3.9 times in the U.S.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.430
and 4.3 times in Canada.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:13.530
TAKESHI KIHARA, Nikkei Asian Review staff
writer)

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:24.130
But, folks, we already talked about Hong Kong’s
housing situation in a previous video, which

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:26.180
I’ll link in the description.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:29.400
And before you jump the gun, I can already
tell you that this has nothing to do with

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:34.240
capitalism gone wild, or corporate greed,
or overpopulation, or anything like that .

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.710
In this case it’s actually the government
who is to blame, because in Hong Kong the

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:41.560
vast majority of the land and a good part
of the houses belong to the government, they

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:42.560
are all public.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.740
But don’t think that’s the end of it,
there’s much, much more.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:50.780
In October 2018, the investment bank Nomura
presented a report warning that the likelihood

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:55.340
that Hong Kong may suffer a financial crisis
in the coming years has soared.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:59.900
“Hong Kong sits in the 'danger zone' for
a fresh financial crisis” Business Insider)

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.200
One of the main reasons for this is that a
good part of the population has taken huge

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.570
loans to buy a home.

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:08.930
Therefore, a very high percentage of Hong
Kong residents, particularly young professionals,

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:12.260
are up to their necks in mortgage debt.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:15.790
Hong Kong has very large credit and property
market overhangs.

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:22.230
Since 2008, real property prices have risen
125% and the ratio of private non-financial

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:26.000
credit to GDP has surged to 299%.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.100
Nomura)

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:31.010
And if we add the fact that wages haven’t
increased and that the economy depends greatly

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:40.210
on China, whose own growth seems to be slowing
down, this could be a recipe for disaster.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:48.300
"I want to leave because Hong Kong is no longer
what it used to be.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:53.330
My plan is to get a master's degree in Canada,
find a job, and start a new life”.Jonathan

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:56.199
Chan, a 24-year-old Hong Kong student.)

00:06:56.199 --> 00:07:00.270
Because of course… killing yourself working
endless days, to live in a tiny house for

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:04.449
which, you pay a huge mortgage, leaving you
very little disposable income… it doesn’t

00:07:04.449 --> 00:07:05.570
really make you feel very optimistic.

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:06.570
Don’t you agree?

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:09.150
And then on top of that you can’t set up
anything on your own because commercial space

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:12.560
is also very expensive, which makes matters
even worse.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:16.240
Well, that's precisely what more and more
young Hong Kong people think...

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.010
Check this out.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:26.639
“According to a 2018 survey by the Chinese
University of Hong Kong, 51% of people between

00:07:26.639 --> 00:07:31.360
the ages of 18 and 30 said they are thinking
about leaving the territory”.

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:34.040
TAKESHI KIHARA, Nikkei staff writer)

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:37.030
More than half of the younger population want
to live somewhere else.

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:38.669
Don’t tell me that that isn’t worrisome.

00:07:38.669 --> 00:07:42.080
And then we need to take into account China’s
increasing political interference and the

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:47.190
growing restrictions on political freedoms:

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:50.830
Hong Kong bans pro-independence party as China
tightens grip.

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:55.090
Hong Kong National Party banned in the interests
of ‘national safety’.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:56.430
The Guardian)

00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:00.121
Hong Kong press freedom in question as FT
journalist denied visa.

00:08:00.121 --> 00:08:01.121
Hong Kong has refused to renew a work visa
for the Asia news editor of the Financial

00:08:01.121 --> 00:08:02.121
Times.

00:08:02.121 --> 00:08:03.121
Reuters)

00:08:03.121 --> 00:08:04.169
As you can see, it wouldn’t be unreasonable
to say that we’re facing something like

00:08:04.169 --> 00:08:13.729
the decline of Hong Kong’s splendor, that
city, folks, which dazzled the world by becoming

00:08:13.729 --> 00:08:17.840
a financial, commercial, and cultural center…

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:23.240
For decades Hong Kong had the third largest
film industry in the world.

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:26.250
An industry that was thought could rival even
Hollywood itself.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:30.530
But hang on just a second… because some
argue that... yes all this has happened, but

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:32.940
now the city is about to experience a rebirth.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:45.329
So are Hong Kong's best days yet to come?

00:08:45.329 --> 00:08:50.999
(THE NEW CHINESE HONG KONG)

00:08:50.999 --> 00:08:55.779
We’ve already told you here on VisualPolitik:
the Chinese president doesn’t really like

00:08:55.779 --> 00:08:58.399
this whole one country two systems thing.

00:08:58.399 --> 00:09:01.589
Xi Jinping is determined to reduce Hong Kong’s
political autonomy.

00:09:01.589 --> 00:09:08.269
Well, to achieve this he has presented a great
plan: the Great Bay metropolitan area project.

00:09:08.269 --> 00:09:13.910
See, the Chinese president wants to turn the
entire Pearl River delta, where cities such

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:19.050
as Hong Kong, Macao, Shenzhen or Guangzhou
are located, into a large metropolitan area

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:22.510
that is perfectly interconnected and has immense
economic integration.

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:27.320
And we are talking about more than 70 million
people and a GDP that in 2030, according to

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:30.529
Morgan Stanley estimates, will be over three
trillion dollars.

00:09:30.529 --> 00:09:34.950
That’s larger than the UK’s economy.

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:38.860
So far, the big infrastructure projects are
already a reality: a high-speed train has

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:43.470
been inaugurated between Hong Kong, Shenzhen
and Guangzhou, and also the longest bridge

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.019
in the world now connects Hong Kong and Macao.

00:09:46.019 --> 00:10:01.019
A connection that is no less than 55 km long,
and which, understandably, cost a fortune.

00:10:01.019 --> 00:10:02.759
The problem?

00:10:02.759 --> 00:10:07.249
The Chinese government wants all this integration
to end Hong Kong’s autonomy.

00:10:07.249 --> 00:10:11.499
And among other things, although they are
formally a single country, regulations, laws

00:10:11.499 --> 00:10:15.910
and courts are different in Hong Kong than
in mainland China There are even borders and

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:20.360
customs between the two territories…

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:24.199
So in order to make the metropolitan area
really work, some harmonization would have

00:10:24.199 --> 00:10:28.559
to take place... which of course means that
Hong Kong would become increasingly Chinese

00:10:28.559 --> 00:10:32.970
and that when its autonomy agreement expires
in 2047, it wouldn’t be renewed without

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:34.480
strict conditions.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:38.329
Do you see it now?

00:10:38.329 --> 00:10:40.639
Well, there's more.

00:10:40.639 --> 00:10:44.699
In a spectacular turn of events, China has
appointed Hong Kong as the National Center

00:10:44.699 --> 00:10:48.689
for Technology and Innovation in southern
China.

00:10:48.689 --> 00:10:54.009
For the first time, the Chinese government
has begun financing research projects in Hong

00:10:54.009 --> 00:10:58.300
Kong’s universities, has demanded the government
enforce a doubling of research and development

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:02.959
budgets and approves a program to attract
scientists and technologists from all over

00:11:02.959 --> 00:11:05.490
the world.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:07.560
But...

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:08.620
Why would China do this?

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:13.540
Well folks, because Hong Kong has a huge advantage
over the rest of the Asian continent: it’s

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:17.420
considered a market economy and isn’t subject
to import restrictions.

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:21.720
That is, Hong Kong companies can buy whatever
they want, including high-tech goods that

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:26.790
can have both civilian and military applications,
products that the government and Chinese companies

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:27.790
cannot buy.

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:31.829
In other words, China wants to take advantage
of Hong Kong's status to boost its technological

00:11:31.829 --> 00:11:32.980
development plans.

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:37.490
If it succeeds, that’ll be great for China
and Hong Kong.But even if that plan is thwarted,

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:39.990
China will still get the upper hand with Hong
Kong.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:43.899
Consider this; if the United States and the
rest of the other Western powers impose restrictions

00:11:43.899 --> 00:11:48.649
on Hong Kong and end their free port status,
this will push the city even more into China’s

00:11:48.649 --> 00:11:54.350
hands, who will be happy to invest billions
in this territory in exchange for full control.

00:11:54.350 --> 00:12:02.089
Anyway, folks, now you see.

00:12:02.089 --> 00:12:08.190
It’s hard to predict if we’re facing Hong
Kong’s decline or rebirth, but what does

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:12.189
seem clear, is that Hong Kong will become
more and more Chinese.

00:12:12.189 --> 00:12:40.139
But now it's your turn, do you think Hong
Kong will see a return to its golden days?

